    content: `**Funeral etiquette**: dark, subdued, conservative clothing remains the broadly safe default (black is traditional in many Western cultures, though this varies significantly by religion and culture — some traditions call for white or other specific colors, so checking the specific family or cultural context matters when uncertain), and arriving a bit early rather than right at the start time is generally considered respectful, since services often begin promptly. When offering condolences, simple, genuine, and brief is almost always better than elaborate — "I'm so sorry for your loss," or sharing a brief, specific, positive memory of the deceased if you knew them, tends to land better than philosophical statements about death or unsolicited advice about grief, which can inadvertently minimize what the grieving person is experiencing. Sending flowers, a sympathy card, or a donation to a charity specified by the family (increasingly common "in lieu of flowers" requests) are all standard, appropriate gestures — checking the specific family's stated preference, when given, is worth honoring over a generic default. **Wedding guest etiquette**: never wear white (or, depending on culture, whatever color is traditionally reserved for the bride specifically) as a guest, since it risks visually competing with or overshadowing the couple on their day — this remains one of the most consistently observed rules across wedding etiquette broadly. RSVP promptly and accurately by the requested deadline, since caterers and venues need firm headcounts well in advance, and a late or non-response creates real logistical difficulty for the couple. Dress according to the stated dress code on the invitation (black-tie, cocktail, casual, etc.) — when no explicit code is given, erring toward slightly more formal than the venue/time suggests is generally safer than under-dressing. Gift etiquette for weddings varies more than funeral etiquette by region and culture — some cultures/families expect a monetary gift (often via a specific customary format like a cash-filled envelope), while others follow a registry-gift tradition — following the couple's stated registry or any explicit cultural norm communicated (directly or through the invitation) is the most reliable way to get this right.`,
